# control85.rc with 64-bit zend_long on 32-bit pointers, and nothing else changed.
#
# It answers ONE question wasm64 cannot: what does PHP_INT_SIZE=8 cost when the
# POINTERS stay 4 bytes. wasm64 buys the same integer width and pays for it in
# every pointer too -- Bucket 24->32, zend_string 20->32, HashTable 44->56,
# zend_object 48->56, for a measured +16.2% blended and 5.00 MiB of margin
# against the 128 MiB isolate. This arm should grow only the hash-bearing
# structs. How much less is the whole decision and nobody has measured it.
#
# DO NOT restate the old pitch that this is "nearly free because zval is 16 on
# both targets". zval does stay 16, and Bucket still goes 24 -> 32: zend_ulong
# is typedef'd from zend_long and both Bucket and zend_string carry a
# zend_ulong h, so widening either the hash or the pointer alone produces 32.
# Modelled with a real compiler before this file existed.
#
# THE FLAG IS A COMPILE FLAG AND NOTHING ELSE. Zend/zend_long.h is the sole
# authority: it sets ZEND_ENABLE_ZVAL_LONG64 from compiler predefines
# (__x86_64__, __LP64__, _LP64, _WIN64) and DERIVES SIZEOF_ZEND_LONG from it.
# Neither configure.ac nor Zend/Zend.m4 mentions either macro, so there is no
# generated header to patch. On wasm32 none of those predefines is set, so the
# guard does not fire and a -D on the command line stands.
#
# Makefile:209 clears EXTRA_CFLAGS after this file is included, so the flag
# cannot live here; src/build-variant.sh greps for the marker below and
# passes -DZEND_ENABLE_ZVAL_LONG64=1 as a make command-line variable.
#
# Score it with worker/scripts/measure/growth-ladder.ts on the AUTHENTICATED
# render. A plain render reads 96.00 MiB on every arm and answers nothing.

# ZEND_LONG64=1

PHP_VERSION=8.5

MAIN_MODULE=0
ASYNCIFY=0
OPTIMIZE=2
INITIAL_MEMORY=96MB

WITH_VRZNO=1
WITH_LIBXML=static
WITH_DOM=static
WITH_SIMPLEXML=static
WITH_XML=static
WITH_YAML=static
WITH_ZLIB=static

WITH_CTYPE=1
WITH_FILTER=1
WITH_SESSION=1
WITH_TOKENIZER=1

WITH_BCMATH=0
WITH_CALENDAR=0
WITH_EXIF=0

WITH_ICONV=0
# 0 ON PURPOSE and mbstring is still IN; see the CONFIGURE_FLAGS line at the bottom.
# WITH_MBSTRING=static emits `--with-mbstring`, and ext/mbstring/config.m4 declares
# PHP_ARG_ENABLE([mbstring]) with no --with- form, so autoconf silently ignores it. Every earlier
# build reported mbstring absent with WITH_MBSTRING=static set.
WITH_MBSTRING=0
WITH_ONIGURUMA=0
WITH_SQLITE=0
WITH_GD=0
WITH_LIBPNG=0
WITH_LIBJPEG=0
WITH_FREETYPE=0
WITH_LIBWEBP=0
WITH_LIBZIP=0
WITH_PHAR=0
WITH_XMLREADER=0
WITH_XMLWRITER=0
WITH_INTL=0
WITH_TIDY=0
WITH_SDL=0
WITH_OPENSSL=0
WITH_PDO_PGLITE=0
WITH_PDO_CFD1=0
WITH_WAITLINE=0

# opcache is compiled in; file_cache_only is set at runtime from the worker ini
CONFIGURE_FLAGS+= --enable-opcache

# THE REAL mbstring. Its last refusal died on a measured +2 ms of edge startup (n=4 interleaved cold
# compiles); bundle and speed were already dead. What it closes: 10 core-reachable parity
# divergences, all mb_convert_encoding against legacy charsets, and ext-mbstring moves from
# POLYFILLED_PLATFORM to NATIVE_PLATFORM so contrib requirements stop degrading to unverifiable.
# --disable-mbregex keeps oniguruma out: mb_substr / mb_strtolower / mb_strlen need libmbfl, which is
# bundled in ext/mbstring, and Drupal core calls no mb_ereg_* function.
CONFIGURE_FLAGS+= --enable-mbstring --disable-mbregex

# The park. src/ext/cfwpark is installed into php-src by src/build-variant.sh before configure runs.
CONFIGURE_FLAGS+= --enable-cfwpark

# A growable table and an exported stack pointer, which together let a -sSIDE_MODULE extension link
# into this binary at request time with no code generation anywhere.
#
# MEASURED, NOT INFERRED, and it inverts what this file used to assume. `build-static.sh`'s header
# says a dynamic build "cannot load .so extensions in Workers at all", so every extension must be
# compiled in. That is true of MAIN_MODULE=1, which leaves JS-library symbols undefined in the GOT
# and reaches `convertJsFunctionToWasm` -- a runtime module build, refused by the codegen ban with
# `Wasm code generation disallowed by embedder`. It is FALSE of this arm. ext/calendar as a
# 19,075-byte side module linked and ran in a workerd isolate where `new WebAssembly.Module` and
# `new Function` were both confirmed refused in the same request: `extension_loaded("calendar")`
# true, `cal_days_in_month(CAL_GREGORIAN,8,2003)` 31.
#
# MAIN_MODULE is not needed for it and costs more than it returns: MM=1 is +13.62% and does not run,
# MM=2 is +0.33% and buys only the removal of four libc shims (time, localtime_r, strlen, mktime),
# which a bundled wasm libc side module removes anyway. This arm is the shipping shape plus two
# bytes.
#
# Both flags are LINK-ONLY. The table is the one the loader grows to place a library's element
# segment, and `__stack_pointer` is one of the five globals a side module imports -- emcc stops
# exporting it by default and the loader cannot place a module without it. Neither changes how any
# object addresses memory, so no compile flag is involved.
#
# THE COST IS NOT ZERO AND IS NOT THE BINARY SIZE. A growable table defeats one V8 optimisation on
# every indirect call: attributed at ~1.1-1.4% steady state on its own round-robin arm (A/A 0.43%),
# against +2 bytes of module and no change to `php_embed_init`, which stays flat at 15.7-17.1 ms.
# Re-measure it here before treating that as this build's number; it was taken on a --disable-all
# build at PHP_INT_SIZE 4, not on this one.

# TABLE_GROWTH=1
